              F
              sw
              ) which has been suggested to represent central sudomotor activity. Sweat gland output (SGO) on each site was calculated by dividing
              m˙
              sw
              by ASG. ASG was significantly higher on the forearm than on the back and thigh, and SGO was significantly lower on the forearm than on the back and thigh. However, ASG and SGO did not significantly change over the day.
              T¯
              b
              and
              T
              oes
              thresholds for the onset of sweating showed a significant change with both the temperature rhythms at rest prior to each procedure, while the slopes of the relationships
              F
